# The law rank/cycle_space.sqlc owes: the layer graph has a loop, ignoring direction, exactly when
# some fusion's parts overlap in what they compose. One row, the two checks held against each
# other.
#
# Which loop
#   Here a loop ignores direction, and rank/graph_measures.sqlc counts it over the edges taken both
#   ways. composition/descent.sqlc's loop follows direction: its `CYCLE` clause stops on a layer
#   that repeats within its own path, so a diamond is not one there. Both are right about the same
#   diamond: nothing composes itself, and a layer is still reached twice. Unqualified, the word
#   names neither, and these queries have a second graph, the unit graph, whose loops are not zero.
#
# Two checks, neither reading the other
#   rank/graph_measures.sqlc counts nodes, edges and pieces, and the loops they give.
#   checks/jagged_layer.sqlc asks a different question of a different relation: whether any layer
#   arrives at a fusion through two paths. They share no code. A loop in the layer graph is a layer
#   arriving twice under one fusion, so the two cannot disagree, and their agreement here is
#   evidence rather than two sentences agreeing.
#
# Zero against more than zero, never the two counts
#   The graph counts independent loops; the rule counts fusions in breach, and one loop can accuse
#   more than one fusion. Holding the numbers equal would pass on the corpus and break on the first
#   document where one loop implicates two fusions.
#
# Testing something is a column, not a comment
#   Nought equals nought is true of an empty graph and of a rule that examined nothing, so the node
#   count, the edge count and the examined count are in `holds`. A graph with no edges has no loops
#   for the wrong reason, and this law refuses to call that a pass.
#
# Two kinds of rule
#   A condition round a loop is the unit graph's, checked by
#   checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close. A balance at a node is the fusion rule's. This law says
#   the composition graph has no loops at all.
#
# Why a correct fusion cannot add a loop
#   A fusion adds one node and one edge per part. Parts drawn from separate pieces of the graph add
#   no loop; two parts inside one piece add one. That is why the rule is about parts overlapping and
#   not about how many parts there are: no correct filing adds a loop.
#
# examples/graphs keeps its own assertion
#   It is the only reader that takes these measures as a program rather than as rows, and it names
#   the change that moves both checks together. Removing it would leave this law as the only
#   witness to its own claim.
-- rank/graph_measures.sqlc's layer row at the corpus scope against checks/jagged_layer.sqlc.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/cycle_space' AS subject,
           x.nodes > 0 AND x.edges > 0 AND x.examined > 0
           AND (x.dim = 0) = (x.violations = 0)                                      AS holds,
           format('%s independent loop(s) over %s node(s), %s edge(s), %s component(s); '
                  '%s of %s fusion(s) examined have parts that overlap',
                  x.dim, x.nodes, x.edges, x.components, x.violations, x.examined)   AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT m.cycle_space_dim AS dim, m.n_nodes AS nodes, m.m_edges AS edges,
               m.c_components AS components, j.violations, j.examined
        FROM      (
            :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
        ) m
        CROSS JOIN (
            SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.violates)             AS violations,
                   count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.violates IS NOT NULL) AS examined
            FROM ( :compose(checks/jagged_layer.sqlc) ) k
        ) j
        WHERE m.graph = 'layers' AND m.filing IS NULL
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'cycle_space'
